# CLAUDE.md

Behavioral guidelines to reduce common LLM coding mistakes. Merge with project-specific instructions as needed.

**Tradeoff:** These guidelines bias toward caution over speed. For trivial tasks, use judgment.

## 1. Think Before Coding

**Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.**

Before implementing:
- State your assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask.
- If multiple interpretations exist, present them - don't pick silently.
- If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted.
- If something is unclear, stop. Name what's confusing. Ask.

## 2. Simplicity First

**Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.**

- No features beyond what was asked.
- No abstractions for single-use code.
- No "flexibility" or "configurability" that wasn't requested.
- No error handling for impossible scenarios.
- If you write 200 lines and it could be 50, rewrite it.

Ask yourself: "Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?" If yes, simplify.

## 3. Surgical Changes

**Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.**

When editing existing code:
- Don't "improve" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.
- Don't refactor things that aren't broken.
- Match existing style, even if you'd do it differently.
- If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it - don't delete it.

When your changes create orphans:
- Remove imports/variables/functions that YOUR changes made unused.
- Don't remove pre-existing dead code unless asked.

The test: Every changed line should trace directly to the user's request.

## 4. Don't Commit on Your Own

**Never run `git commit` (or `git push`) unless I explicitly ask you to.**

- Make and stage changes freely; leave committing to me.
- When work is ready, say so and let me review the diff and commit it myself.
- This holds even mid-task, even when a workflow or skill would normally commit automatically — surface the finished changes instead of committing them.

## 5. Goal-Driven Execution

**Define success criteria. Loop until verified.**

Transform tasks into verifiable goals:
- "Add validation" → "Write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass"
- "Fix the bug" → "Write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass"
- "Refactor X" → "Ensure tests pass before and after"

For multi-step tasks, state a brief plan:
```
1. [Step] → verify: [check]
2. [Step] → verify: [check]
3. [Step] → verify: [check]
```

Strong success criteria let you loop independently. Weak criteria ("make it work") require constant clarification.

---

**These guidelines are working if:** fewer unnecessary changes in diffs, fewer rewrites due to overcomplication, and clarifying questions come before implementation rather than after mistakes.

# sts2-rl

A pure-Python simulation of **Slay the Spire 2 combat**, wrapped in a Gymnasium
environment, with the goal of training PyTorch models to play the game. The
current training path is a **raw-PyTorch masked PPO** loop (`train_torch.py` +
`sts2_rl/models.py`); the Stable-Baselines3 / MaskablePPO path (`train.py`, full
combat env) is now a legacy reference.

For the end-to-end RL stack (env → network → PPO loop), see
[RL_ARCHITECTURE.md](RL_ARCHITECTURE.md). For a file-by-file reader's guide to
the `sts2_rl/` core modules, see
[MODULES.md](MODULES.md). For the engine-side decision interfaces an env must
respect (action space, `card_selector`, what to read off `CombatState`), see
[RL.md](RL.md). For the normative observation contract, see
[OBS_SCHEMA.md](OBS_SCHEMA.md); [OBS_PLAN.md](OBS_PLAN.md) is the history of
how that observation was designed (all phases shipped).

## The golden rule: fidelity to the decompiled source

The decompiled STS2 game source lives in the sibling directory
`c:\Users\Perry\Desktop\Slay the Spire 2` (C#, Godot). It is the **source of
truth** for all game mechanics. When implementing or changing combat logic,
check the real implementation first:

| Topic | Where to look in the game source |
|---|---|
| Hooks (events/modifiers/predicates) | `src/Core/Hooks/Hook.cs`, `src/Core/Models/AbstractModel.cs` |
| Damage/block pipeline, damage typing | `src/Core/Commands/DamageCmd.cs`, `src/Core/ValueProps/` |
| Effect verbs (heal, stun, escape, ...) | `src/Core/Commands/CreatureCmd.cs`, `PlayerCmd.cs`, `CardCmd.cs` |
| Monster stats, moves, AI | `src/Core/Models/Monsters/<Name>.cs` |
| Events | `src/Core/Models/EventModel.cs`, `src/Core/Models/Events/`; per-act pools in `src/Core/Models/Acts/<Act>.cs` (AllEvents) |
| Enchantments | `src/Core/Models/EnchantmentModel.cs`, `src/Core/Models/Enchantments/` |
| Move state machines & intents | `src/Core/MonsterMoves/` |
| Powers | `src/Core/Models/Powers/` (276 of them) |
| Relics | `src/Core/Models/Relics/`; per-character pools in `src/Core/Models/RelicPools/` |
| Potions | `src/Core/Models/Potions/` |
| Turn structure | `src/Core/Combat/CombatManager.cs`, `PlayerTurnPhase.cs` |

Numbers (HP ranges, damage, stacks) should be taken from the source models —
note that many are wrapped in `AscensionHelper.GetValueIfAscension(...)`; this
sim uses the **non-ascension** values.

## Commands

```powershell
# Run all tests (from repo root; uses the `py` launcher on this machine)
py -m pytest test/ -q

# Train (current path): raw-PyTorch masked PPO (saves runs/sts2_torch.pt)
py train_torch.py                                      # combat env, Act-1 pool
py train_torch.py --encounter fuzzy_wurm_weak --timesteps 500000
py train_torch.py --resume runs/sts2_torch.pt          # continue
py train_torch.py --env run                            # FULL-RUN env (map/events/
                                                       # shops/rewards + combat;
                                                       # saves runs/sts2_run_torch.pt)
py train_torch.py --env column                         # phase-1 curriculum: full runs
                                                       # on randomized single-column
                                                       # maps, floor-only reward (saves
                                                       # runs/sts2_column_torch.pt; its
                                                       # checkpoint resumes on --env run)
py train_torch.py --arch entset                        # (the default) EntitySetActorCritic,
                                                       # the v4-native arch — masked per-row
                                                       # embeddings over the {f,i} obs Dict
                                                       # (models.py); --arch mlp/entity are
                                                       # refused against the current envs
                                                       # (unnormalized ids would swamp the
                                                       # numeric features — checkpoints.py);
                                                       # checkpoints are arch-stamped —
                                                       # switching arch = full retrain
py train_torch.py --anneal-lr                          # decay lr 3e-4 -> 0 over THIS
                                                       # invocation's iterations (a resume
                                                       # restarts the schedule over its own
                                                       # --timesteps budget)
# Stability defaults: --target-kl 0.02 early-stops the epoch loop on the run-scale
# envs (--env run|column) when the RUNNING MEAN of the current epoch's approx_kl
# exceeds it, scored after every minibatch (not at the epoch boundary — a whole
# epoch at 6x the target is enough to permanently cost a trained policy several
# reward points); --env combat keeps it off. Pass --target-kl 0 to disable, or
# --ent-coef-final to anneal the entropy bonus (default: constant at --ent-coef).

# Resuming after an ENV CHANGE that moves the return distribution (a new reward
# term, a rule change like a per-act heal): the critic is stale, so its
# advantages are mis-signed and a full-LR resume spends them wrecking a good
# policy. Warm the value head up first — the actor is untouched (kl is exactly
# 0.0000 for those iterations, logged as [critic-warmup]):
py train_torch.py --resume runs/sts2_run_torch_v4.pt --critic-warmup 20

# Unattended long runs: every save is atomic (<path>.tmp + os.replace), each
# periodic save also writes an iter-stamped snapshot <stem>.iter000123.pt
# (last --keep-snapshots kept, default 5) plus <stem>.best.pt on the best
# 100-episode --best-metric (win for combat/run, ep_ret for column — the
# exploring policy's own metric, not a greedy eval), and every iteration
# appends a row to <stem>.csv. Checkpoints carry global_step, so a resume
# continues the step count, the CSV, and fresh env seeds. --lr defaults to
# None: on a resume the optimizer's saved LR is kept unless --lr is passed.
# --n-envs/--n-steps follow the same rule (defaults 32/512): checkpoints record
# the rollout geometry, so a bare --resume reproduces the batch that trained
# the weights instead of silently reverting it. Each field resolves
# independently (explicit flag > checkpoint > default), and the resolved
# "N envs x M steps (batch B)" line is printed at startup.
py train_torch.py --keep-snapshots 0 --best-metric none  # live checkpoint + CSV only

# Train (legacy reference): SB3 MaskablePPO on the full combat env (saves sts2_full.zip)
py train.py
# Minimal toy-env SB3 trainer (env.py Strike/Defend only, saves sts2_ppo.zip)
py test/rl.py

# Evaluate a saved model (win rate + final-HP stats over N episodes;
# --env full adds lethal-probe accuracy, --baselines adds baseline rows).
# A *.pt model is a train_torch.py checkpoint (arch/schema/env read from its
# own stamps, greedy by default); anything else loads as an SB3 zip.
py eval.py sts2_ppo --episodes 1000                    # legacy SB3, toy env
py eval.py --env full --baselines                      # no model: random + oracle
py eval.py runs/sts2_torch.pt --env full               # torch combat ckpt: win% + probes
py eval.py runs/sts2_column_torch.pt --env column --episodes 50 --baselines
py eval.py runs/sts2_run_torch.pt --env run --sample   # sample instead of argmax
# Run-scale rows report win%, mean/median max floor, acts reached, HP on wins,
# decisions/episode, and the death-floor distribution. Deterministic per --seed.

# Per-encounter breakdown of a torch combat checkpoint (all non-boss Act-1 fights)
py test/eval.py --model runs/sts2_torch.pt --episodes 50

# migrate_ckpt.py is retired: the phase-1 {"f", "i"} Dict rewrite is not a
# feature-only schema bump, so nothing can be migrated across it. Running it
# always exits with an error explaining why. Pre-v4 checkpoints need a retrain.

# Observation ablation: train + compare full vs numeric-ablated obs arms
py test/ablation.py

# Interactive terminal combat demo (pick encounter by editing ENCOUNTER in the file)
py test/run.py
